For a curve C in space:
Work = \int_C \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{r} = \int_C P \, dx + Q \, dy + R \, dz
 
To evaluate: parameterize C, express x, y, z and dx, dy, dz in terms of the parameter.
 
 
 
Ex 1:
\vec{F} = \langle yz, xz, xy \rangle
 
C: x = t^3, y = t^2, z = t, 0 \le t \le 1
dx = 3t^2 dt, dy = 2t dt, dz = dt
 

\begin{align*}
\int_C \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{r} &= \int_C yz \, dx + xz \, dy + xy \, dz \\
&= \int_0^1 t^3 \cdot 3t^2 dt + t^4 \cdot 2t dt + t^5 dt \\
&= \int_0^1 6t^5 dt = \left[ t^6 \right]_0^1 = 1
\end{align*}

 
 
 
Ex 2:
Same \vec{F}, curve C' = C_1 + C_2 + C_3
 
- For C_1, C_2: in the xy-plane, z = 0, dz = 0
\Rightarrow \int yz \, dx + xz \, dy + xy \, dz = 0
- For C_3: x = 1, dx = 0; y = 1, dy = 0; z from 0 to 1

\int_{C_3} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{r} = \int_{C_3} xy \, dz = \int_0^1 1 \, dz = 1

 
Thus:
\int_{C'} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{r} = \int_{C_1} + \int_{C_2} + \int_{C_3} = 0 + 0 + 1 = 1
 
